  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1205 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nA weakened/suppressed ridge aloft will be over the southern part of \nTexas and the adjacent part of northeastern Mexico Monday into \nTuesday. Models hint at a possibility of a weak disturbance or two \nmoving around its northern periphery Monday and Tuesday. Trailing \nportion of a cold front is forecast to move south into the Big \nCountry on Monday, and into the central part of our area Monday \nnight before stalling. With a combination of weak upper support, \nsurface frontal boundary and possible leftover outflow boundaries \nfrom previous convection, much of our area has low to medium chances \n(20-50%) for showers and thunderstorms Monday through Tuesday and \ninto Tuesday night. A few strong storms will be possible in the \nafternoon/early evening hours with localized downburst winds and \nfrequent lightning the primary hazards. The airmass will be moist \nand with precipitable water values 1.4 to 1.8 inches, locally heavy \nrainfall will be possible. Temperatures Monday and Tuesday will be a \nlittle cooler but still above normal for September 21-22. For the \nmost part expect highs to be in the lower to mid 90s Monday and \nupper 80s to mid 90s Tuesday. Lows will be in the upper 60s and \nlower 70s. Will be monitoring subsequent model trends for the \npossibility of highs to be a little cooler than currently forecast \nin the Big Country behind the front. \n\nThe upper high is forecast to strengthen a little and be centered \nover the Texas coast Wednesday and Thursday into Friday. By \nSaturday, the high is progged to be centered over Deep South Texas. \nWhile an isolated shower/thunderstorm cannot be ruled out mainly in \nour northwest and northern counties, the higher rain chances are \nexpected to be in West Texas toward the Red River with mostly dry \nconditions for our area. Highs are expected to be several degrees \nabove normal in the lower to mid 90s.",
